Ignitition and combustion of solids happens on their surfaces.
The more finely ground, the more oxygen can reach the surface
per kilogram of stuff burning.  This is why a block of solid
aluminum is very hard to burn - Oxide forms a barier - but very
finely powdered alumimum is used in explosives and incendiaries.

Why sugar or flour or whatever is involved in explosions - It burns
so get it to burn fast enough and it counts as an explosion.

Why ash is involved in explosions - Ash is already an oxide, but
different oxides have different binding energies.   Consider that
thermite is iron oxide mixed with aluminum powder.  Once
ignited the aluminum draws the oxygen from the iron oxide.  If
you are careful enough to pick the right oxide minerals of ash
you can make a thermite style reaction.


